@if (Visible)
{
    <div class="modal fade show d-block" tabindex="-1" role="dialog" @onclick="OnClose">
        <div class="modal-dialog modal-lg modal-dialog-scrollable" role="document" @onclick:stopPropagation>
            <div class="modal-content">
                <div class="modal-header">
                    <h5 class="modal-title">How the Commander Builder works</h5>
                    <button type="button" class="btn-close" @onclick="OnClose"></button>
                </div>
                <div class="modal-body">

                    <p class="text-muted">
                        The builder runs a staged pipeline that consults the AI at exactly two points.
                        Everything else is deterministic code.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Two pathways are available. <strong>Guided</strong> lets you pick archetypes and themes —
                        the tool derives coverage targets from those generic traits. <strong>Custom</strong> lets
                        you describe the deck in your own words instead, without breaking it into preset categories.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Choose your commander(s)</h6>
                    <p>
                        Your commander is the single most important input — it determines the color identity the
                        entire 99 must respect and shapes how the AI classifies every card. Partner pairs are
                        fully supported; selecting two commanders reduces the available slots from 99 to 98.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">Must-include cards</h6>
                    <p>
                        Cards added to the Must-Include list are locked into the deck before the fill pass runs.
                        Use <strong>Validate Cards</strong> to check each name against Scryfall before building —
                        unrecognized names block the build entirely. Locked cards are excluded from budget
                        enforcement and are never candidates for cut suggestions.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">1. Pool gathering</h6>
                    <p>
                        Card data — rules text, mana cost, color identity, and legality — is sourced from Scryfall.
                        Recommendations are then pulled from EDHREC for your commander and each of your chosen themes,
                        and Commander Spellbook near-miss combo pieces — cards one step away from completing a combo
                        with your commander — are also included. Cards outside your color identity or on the EDH ban
                        list are removed before anything else happens.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">2. Template resolution</h6>
                    <p>
                        Your archetype and theme weights are blended into per-role coverage targets: how many Ramp
                        slots, how much Disruption, how many Tutors, and so on. This is deterministic math with no
                        AI involved. Archetype weights shift the balance — Combo raises Tutor density, Control raises
                        Disruption, Aggro lowers the curve. Theme weights determine which card types are favored
                        within each role.
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        If you set a budget, it takes effect here. A per-card price cap excludes candidates above
                        that price from selection. A total deck budget acts as a soft constraint — if no affordable
                        card can fill a role, the best available option is used and flagged in the results so you
                        know what to swap.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">3. Card classification <span class="badge bg-secondary fw-normal ms-1" style="font-size: 0.7em;">AI step 1</span></h6>
                    <p>
                        Every card in the pool is assigned a primary role (Ramp, Card Advantage, Tutor, etc.)
                        and optional secondary roles for cards that contribute to more than one function. Each
                        role assignment also carries a fractional weight — a card that primarily ramps but also
                        draws might count as 1.0 toward Ramp and 0.5 toward Card Advantage. These weights are
                        why the Coverage Report shows decimal values rather than whole numbers.
                        The cheapest model available for your chosen provider handles this step — classification
                        is a straightforward pattern-matching task that does not require advanced reasoning, and
                        the cheapest model is more than capable of it. Results are cached across builds, so most
                        cards are only classified once.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">4. Card selection <span class="badge bg-secondary fw-normal ms-1" style="font-size: 0.7em;">AI step 2</span></h6>
                    <p>
                        For each role, the AI ranks the classified candidates for your specific commander and build
                        direction. The fill engine then picks the top-ranked cards until the coverage target for
                        that role is met. Each selected card gets a written rationale explaining why it belongs in
                        this deck. Your chosen model is used here — this is where the reasoning level you selected
                        actually makes a difference.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">5. Color-fixing</h6>
                    <p>
                        Some basic land slots are upgraded to non-basic color-fixing lands from the pool, ranked
                        by how much mana fixing they provide for the specific colors your deck needs.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">6. Basic land distribution</h6>
                    <p>
                        Remaining basic land slots are split across your colors proportional to pip demand in the 99.
                        A heavily green deck gets more Forests than Mountains even if it runs both colors.
                    </p>

                    <h6 class="fw-semibold mt-3">7. Coverage Report</h6>
                    <p class="mb-0">
                        The first tab in the results shows overlap-aware role coverage vs. the planned targets
                        from the template. Because cards carry fractional weights across multiple roles, the
                        totals show decimal values and can legitimately exceed 99. Roles that fall short of
                        their target are highlighted so you can see where the pool was thin.
                    </p>

                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <div class="modal-backdrop fade show"></div>
}
